# Business Plan Reference

=== layout: one-panel

# Rubric At A Glance

@body

| Section | Weight |
| --- | --- |
| Cover page and table of contents | 5% |
| Executive summary | 30% |
| Science and technology background | 15% |
| Market opportunity | 20% |
| Management team | 5% |
| Investment summary | 5% |
| Overall style | 20% |
| **Total** | **100%** |

=== layout: section

# Writing the Business Plan

=== layout: two-panels

# Cover and Executive Summary

@left

## Cover page and TOC: 5%

- Exactly one page.
- Use a cool, effective logo and company name.
- Include a fake address and contact information.
- Put a clean table of contents on the same page.

@right

## Executive summary: 30%

- Exactly one page; check it again after uploading to TurnItIn.
- Make it a standalone, exciting summary.
- Address the problem, background, analysis, solution, and future outlook.

=== layout: one-panel

# Science and Technology Background

@body

## Required Scope: 15%

- Use at least two content pages.
- Write for the rest of the class; define technical terms and avoid unnecessary jargon.
- Treat this as a science proposal, not a fake lab report.
- Explain the problem in enough detail to show why it matters.

=== layout: one-panel

# Science and Technology: Make the Mechanism Persuasive

@body

- Explain the science or technology, its course connection, and how your company uses it.
- Include a figure when it helps explain the science.
- Share the needed details; do not hide behind trade secrets.
- Identify planned intellectual property.
- Compare with existing methods: cheaper, more efficient, or better for an unmet need.

=== layout: two-panels

# Market Opportunity

@left

## Required scope: 20%

- Use more than one page.
- State what the company sells and the value it brings.
- Define the niche and what makes the company distinct.
- Establish demand, including disease prevalence when relevant.

@right

## Make the market believable

- Identify the unmet market need.
- Compare direct and indirect competition.
- Explain how intellectual property supports an advantage.
- Describe a sales strategy: clients, access, and differentiation.
- Introduce development milestones.

=== layout: one-panel

# Management Team and Investment Summary

@body

## Management Team: 5%

- Use about one page for company history and philosophy.
- Have fun, but within reason. Keep an undergraduate character real to life; ambitious degrees are fine, time travelers are not.
- Explain each person's contribution to milestones and motivation.
- Optional: professional photos and an organization chart showing who is responsible for what.

=== layout: one-panel

# Investment Summary

@body

## Investment Summary: 5%

- Offer funding ideas.
- Show a timeline with at least three milestones.
- Include a financial table with projected revenue and expenses.
- A hockey-stick revenue figure can make the future case visible.

=== layout: one-panel

# Overall Style and Frequent Mistakes

@body

- Overall style is 20%: use effective font and paragraph spacing, white space, and strong figures, graphs, and tables.
- Use at least 11-point body text. Use full justification for paragraphs.
- Do not use Comic Sans or Papyrus for paragraphs. NIH-style sans serif fonts (Arial or Helvetica) and business serif fonts (Times or similar) are reliable choices.
- Use the provided headings; do not make up replacements.
- A weak executive summary that misses key areas or a background section without background loses the reader early.

